package com.ai.rules;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.function.Predicate;

public class RuleEngine {

    // Rule interface with condition and action
    public interface Rule {
        boolean condition(Context context);
        void action(Context context);
    }

    // Context holding data for evaluation
    public static class Context {
        public int userAge;
        public boolean allowAccess = false;
    }

    private final List<Rule> rules = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addRule(Rule rule) {
        rules.add(rule);
    }

    public void evaluate(Context context) {
        for (Rule rule : rules) {
            if (rule.condition(context)) {
                rule.action(context);
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RuleEngine engine = new RuleEngine();

        // Rule: If user is older than 18, allow access
        engine.addRule(new Rule() {
            @Override
            public boolean condition(Context ctx) {
                return ctx.userAge > 18;
            }

            @Override
            public void action(Context ctx) {
                ctx.allowAccess = true;
                System.out.println("Access granted.");
            }
        });

        Context ctx = new Context();
        ctx.userAge = 20;

        engine.evaluate(ctx);
        System.out.println("Allow Access: " + ctx.allowAccess);
    }
}
Common Misunderstandings and Mistakes
Rule Explosion:
As complexity grows, the number of rules can explode, making maintenance difficult.

Overlapping Rules:
Without proper conflict resolution, multiple rules may contradict or cause unintended behavior.

Lack of Learning:
Pure rule-based systems do not learn from data; they require manual updates to rules.

Performance:
Naive rule evaluation can be inefficient; optimized inference engines or indexing are needed for large rule sets.

Summary
The Rules Principle is about encoding domain knowledge as explicit conditional logic.
It enables transparent, interpretable AI decision-making.
Widely used in expert systems, business automation, and policy enforcement.
Requires careful design of rule management, evaluation, and conflict resolution.
Forms a foundational pillar in AI-enhanced software architecture for deterministic reasoning.

Where AI lives (backend vs frontend)

Understanding where AI "lives" in a software system—whether on the backend or frontend—is crucial for designing scalable, efficient, and user-friendly AI-powered applications. This discussion dives deep into the architectural roles AI plays on both sides, their trade-offs, and best practices.

Overview
Backend AI: AI models and logic run on servers or cloud infrastructure.
Frontend AI: AI runs directly in the user's device/browser or client app.
1. AI on the Backend
What it means:
AI models are hosted on centralized servers or cloud platforms.
The frontend sends data (e.g., user input, images) to the backend.
Backend processes data using AI models and returns results.
Typical AI backend components:
Model training pipelines (offline or online)
Model serving APIs (REST, gRPC)
Data storage and preprocessing
Scalability and load balancing infrastructure
Advantages:
Powerful compute resources: Backend can leverage GPUs, TPUs, and distributed clusters.
Security: Sensitive data and models stay protected on servers.
Model complexity: Supports large, complex models (e.g., GPT, BERT).
Centralized updates: Easier to update/improve AI models without client-side deployment.
Data aggregation: Backend can aggregate data from multiple users to improve models.
Challenges:
Latency: Network round-trip time can cause delays.
Scalability: High traffic requires scalable infrastructure.
Cost: Cloud compute and storage can be expensive.
Real-world examples:
Google Search ranking algorithms
Amazon product recommendations
ChatGPT API serving large language models
Fraud detection systems in banking
2. 🖥️ AI on the Frontend
What it means:
AI runs directly on the client device (browser, mobile app, desktop).
Models are often smaller or optimized for edge devices.
No or minimal server interaction needed for inference.
Typical frontend AI components:
On-device ML models (TensorFlow.js, ONNX.js, CoreML, TFLite)
Real-time user interaction and feedback loops
Privacy-preserving AI (data never leaves device)
Advantages:
Low latency: Instant AI inference without network delay.
Offline capability: Works without internet connection.
Privacy: User data stays on device.
Reduced backend load: Less server compute required.
Challenges:
Limited compute power: Devices have limited CPU/GPU.
Model size constraints: Models must be small and efficient.
Security: Models can be reverse engineered or tampered with.
Update complexity: Updating models requires app updates or clever patching.
Real-world examples:
Face detection and filters in camera apps
Autocomplete and spellcheck in browsers
Voice assistants running locally (e.g., Apple Siri offline mode)
AI-powered accessibility features (screen readers, text-to-speech)
3. ⚖️ Backend vs Frontend AI: When to Use Which?
Criteria	Backend AI	Frontend AI
Model complexity	Large, complex models	Lightweight, optimized models
Latency sensitivity	Moderate (network delay expected)	Very low latency needed
Privacy concerns	Data sent to server	Data stays on device
Connectivity	Requires internet	Can work offline
Compute resources	High (cloud GPUs/TPUs)	Limited (mobile CPU/GPU)
Update frequency	Frequent, centralized	Less frequent, client-side deployment needed
Cost	Higher infrastructure cost	Lower backend cost, but device-dependent
4. Hybrid Approaches: Best of Both Worlds
Modern AI architectures often combine backend and frontend AI:

Edge AI with backend fallback: Run lightweight models on device for instant results; fallback to backend for heavy processing.
On-device preprocessing: Frontend preprocesses data (e.g., feature extraction) before sending to backend.
Federated learning: Train models across many devices locally; aggregate updates on backend without raw data transfer.
Progressive enhancement: Basic AI features on frontend; advanced features powered by backend.
